  "final_text": "The dashboard and the alert rule both exist, and the rule is enabled. Its status is still \"pending\", because it hasn't run yet. I didn't open the dashboard to check how the panels render.\n\n**What the data holds:** The Kibana Sample Data Logs view covers web-server requests. The fields I used are `timestamp`, `response` (a string field, so values like \"503\"), `bytes`, `extension`, `clientip` and `geo.*`. The rest are `machine.os`, `agent`, `referer`, `url` and `request`.\n\n**Dashboard:** \"EFF-ef306c8c Web logs overview\" (id `eff-ef306c8c-web-logs-overview-557467553fa5`), with a default range of the last 7 days. It has four panels:\n- Total requests, as a single number.\n- Requests over time, as a line chart.\n- Responses by status code, as a pie chart.\n- Bytes by file extension, as a bar chart.\n\n**Alert rule:** \"EFF-ef306c8c 5xx spike\" (id `d2a727f4-a05e-4251-b894-ae0dfcb6ba01`) is an Elasticsearch query rule on `kibana_sample_data_logs`. It runs every minute and counts documents whose `response.keyword` starts with \"5\" over the last 5 minutes. It fires when that count is above 10. Because there is no action connector, it only records alerts in Kibana and won't send a notification.",
